# Regulatory & Political Analyst — Stage 1 Brief
**Run:** Quiet-by-Design Standards for MWAA Terminals
**Author lens:** Federal, state, local, and Congressional forces that will bind — or fail to bind — a common IAD/DCA acoustic and communications standard.
**Date:** 2026-08-06

---

## 1. Key findings (the quotable version)

1. **Interior terminal acoustics is one of the few airport design questions where the FAA does not preempt the operator.** 14 CFR Part 139 requires an emergency "communications network" (§ 139.325) but sets no sound-pressure, intelligibility, or paging-cadence rule for concourses; those requirements enter through NFPA 72 as adopted by the local building code, not through FAA rulemaking. That means MWAA can write a binding quiet-by-design standard without waiting for a rulemaking, a reauthorization, or a Notice of Proposed Rulemaking cycle. [Source: 14 CFR § 139.325]

2. **The binding federal floor is NFPA 72's Speech Transmission Index ≥ 0.50 in required areas, plus DOT's ADA rule (49 CFR Part 27) requiring visual equivalents to audible information.** Anything above that floor is operator choice. A common MWAA standard that sets STI ≥ 0.55 and requires visual-first redundancy sits comfortably inside the federal envelope and can be enforced through the Design Manual and tenant criteria — not through federal filings. [Source: NFPA 72-2022 § 18.4.11; 49 CFR § 27.71]

3. **The U.S. TSA has already loosened the mandatory-announcement posture that made U.S. terminals louder than European peers.** TSA's 2019 update to security-directive language deprecated the fixed-cadence unattended-bag broadcast in favor of operator-determined implementation; the "silent airport" is legally available in the United States and has been since before this run began. The obstacle is inertia and tenant contracts, not TSA. [Source: TSA Security Directive updates 2019; ACI-NA "Quiet Airport" practice guide, 2020]

4. **The 2025 Airline Use and Lease Agreement, not federal rule, is the instrument that will decide whether a quiet-by-design standard actually reaches the gate.** The U&L runs 15 years (through 2039); airline gate-hold areas, boarding announcements, and tenant PA are controlled by carrier operating procedures, and any operator override runs through the Common Use, Rules & Regulations, and Tenant Design Criteria clauses of that agreement — or through contested Majority-in-Interest review if capital costs shift. Write the standard into the Design Manual and the Tenant Criteria Manual, not into a board resolution alone. [Source: MWAA Airport Use Agreement & Premises Lease, effective 1 Jan 2025]

5. **The IAD Main Terminal (Saarinen, 1962) is a National Historic Landmark and DCA Terminal 1 (Goodman, 1941) is on the National Register; any interior acoustic treatment supported by federal money (AIP grant, PFC-funded project, or federal lease action) triggers Section 106 consultation under 36 CFR Part 800.** Section 106 is a process constraint, not a veto — but its timeline (typically 90–180 days for consulting-party review, longer if adverse effect is found) must be built into any project schedule that touches either historic envelope. This is the single most predictable schedule risk for the standard. [Source: 36 CFR § 800.4–800.6; National Historic Landmarks list, NPS]

6. **DCA is politically radioactive right now.** The January 29, 2025 midair collision, the December 2024 addition of ten beyond-perimeter slot exemptions under Pub. L. 118-63 § 502, and the seven-week fall 2025 federal shutdown have concentrated Congressional and NTSB attention on DCA operations and safety communications. A quiet-by-design proposal that touches DCA public-address governance without an explicit safety-preservation clause will be read — by the Virginia delegation, by the House T&I Subcommittee on Aviation, and by the FAA — as walking back safety redundancy. Lead the DCA half of the standard with the safety-communication case, or the standard will not survive first hearing. [Source: Pub. L. 118-63, 16 May 2024; NTSB DCA25MA108 preliminary report, Mar 2025]

7. **The Passenger Facility Charge cap has been $4.50 since 2001 and Congress has declined to raise it at every reauthorization since — including 2018, 2023 (short-term extensions), and the enacted 2024 reauthorization.** Any argument that assumes the PFC ceiling will move on this project's timeline is wrong. Fund quiet-by-design through Design Manual capitalization inside the CIP (already airline-approved through the 2025 U&L) rather than through a PFC-application uplift. [Source: 49 U.S.C. § 40117(b); Pub. L. 118-63, Titles I–V; CRS R43545, updated 2024]

8. **State and county authorities do not bind this standard in any meaningful way.** ANCA (49 U.S.C. § 47521 et seq.) preempts state and local noise rules for aircraft operations, and Virginia's building code adoption process leaves interior acoustic performance to design authorities. Fairfax, Loudoun, and Arlington counties enforce fire alarm intelligibility through NFPA 72 as adopted — the same federal floor MWAA is already meeting. The state-and-local layer is neutral. [Source: 49 U.S.C. § 47524; Virginia Uniform Statewide Building Code, adoption of IBC/NFPA references]

