## 2. What binds, what doesn't, and on what timeline

### What is **not** a binding constraint on this decision

- **FAA rulemaking.** No pending FAA NPRM addresses terminal-interior acoustics, paging cadence, or PA zoning. FAA AC 150/5360-13B (Airport Terminal Planning) treats acoustic environment as a planning consideration, not a compliance item. The FAA's regulatory interest ends at the emergency-communications network required by § 139.325.
- **TSA design directive.** TSA's Checkpoint Design Guide (v.7, 2024) governs checkpoint layout, CT scanner clearances, credential authentication (CAT-2), and queue geometry; it does not prescribe ambient sound levels or announcement scripting inside or outside the checkpoint. TSA will accept a quieter checkpoint zone if operator absorbs the passenger-communication burden.
- **CBP FIS design.** CBP's Airport Technical Design Standards (2022 revision) prescribes FIS-hall square footage per peak passenger, primary-inspection booth counts, and biometric equipment clearances; the standard sets minimum PA and signage requirements for CBP-controlled process communication but does not preempt operator acoustic standards in adjacent non-sterile space.
- **Airport Improvement Program eligibility.** AIP formula and discretionary grants (Pub. L. 118-63 authorized $4.0B/year through FY2028) can fund acoustic treatment inside eligible terminal projects; there is no AIP prohibition on quiet-by-design elements. AIP eligibility is a paperwork constraint, not a policy constraint.
- **NEPA.** Interior acoustic and PA specifications inside an already-scoped terminal project are almost always covered by the project's existing categorical exclusion or environmental assessment. NEPA re-scoping risk is low unless the standard drives a footprint change.

### What **does** bind, and when

| Constraint | Instrument | Airport-authority decision it touches | Realistic timeframe to unblock |
|---|---|---|---|
| Section 106 historic review | 36 CFR Part 800 | Any federally-assisted work inside Saarinen Main Terminal or DCA T1 | 90–180 days per project; longer with adverse-effect finding |
| Airline consent to tenant PA and gate-hold changes | 2025 U&L, Tenant Design Criteria, Rules & Regulations | Concourse C/D replacement scope; Concourse E opening standards | Negotiated per project; MII trigger if capital cost per enplanement shifts |
| NFPA 72 EVACS intelligibility | Local building code adoption (VA/MD/DC) | Every renovated space | Compliance-check at commissioning; no policy timeline |
| DOT ADA effective-communication | 49 CFR Part 27; DOJ 2010 ADA Standards | Every public space | Continuous; enforced by complaint |
| Congressional attention on DCA safety optics | Political, not statutory | Any DCA announcement-reduction proposal | Indefinite; sensitivity peaks through 2027 NTSB final report cycle |
| Next FAA reauthorization | Pub. L. 118-63 expires 30 Sep 2028 | Nothing directly on this standard, but the political window for federal aviation policy attention closes early 2028 | Bank the standard before reauthorization heat |

