## 4. Case studies where politics or regulation determined outcome

**a. DCA perimeter rule as the canonical politically-durable inefficiency.** Every credible economic analysis since the 1986 handoff has concluded the 1,250-mile perimeter is inefficient. It has moved four times — 2000 (AIR-21), 2003, 2012, and 2024 — always by adding a handful of exemptions, never by repeal. The 2024 addition of 10 beyond-perimeter exemptions (Pub. L. 118-63 § 502) was pitched, debated, and passed as a Senate courtesy exercise among home-state members, not as aviation policy. Read this as the base rate for federally-scoped DCA policy fights: they end in narrow accommodations, not repeal. Any quiet-by-design provision that requires federal-statutory change is dead on arrival. [Source: 49 U.S.C. § 41714; Pub. L. 118-63 § 502]

**b. LGA and JFK slot controls at the FAA administrative order level.** JFK and LGA slot rules have been governed by successive FAA orders since 2008; each attempt to move to a market-clearing mechanism has been withdrawn under Congressional pressure. This tells the MWAA strategist that even administratively-set federal restrictions on airport operations behave like statute once a Congressional delegation adopts them. DCA slot posture will not change on this project's timeline. [Source: FAA Order 7210.802; GAO-12-341]

**c. Section 106 at TWA Flight Center / JFK.** The Saarinen-designed TWA head-house adaptive-reuse project (2016–2019) went through full Section 106 consultation with New York SHPO; the process took approximately 14 months from initiation to Memorandum of Agreement and required the developer to preserve specific interior acoustic and visual elements. This is the near-perfect precedent for what a Saarinen Main Terminal interior acoustic project at IAD will look like: workable, but not fast, and requires a preservation-consultant scope in the design contract. [Source: NY SHPO Project Review 15PR03921]

**d. San Diego International "quiet zone" ambient program (2014–2018).** SAN adopted operator-driven announcement reduction and directional-speaker deployment under its Terminal 2 West expansion without any federal filing beyond standard NEPA project documentation. Result: measured reduction in announcement count and no TSA, FAA, or DOT enforcement action. Direct precedent that a US Part 139 airport can implement quiet-by-design under existing federal envelope. [Source: SAN Airport Authority Environmental Documents, 2015; ACI-NA case study, 2019]

