## 6. What to tell the Strategist plainly

Quiet-by-design at MWAA is **not** a political problem. It is a design-manual, tenant-criteria, and airline-consent problem sitting inside an unusually favorable regulatory frame. The FAA does not preempt this space; TSA already allows it; NFPA and ADA set a floor MWAA already meets; ANCA does not reach interior terminal acoustics; state and county authorities are neutral.

The three things that will actually move on operator-controllable timeframes:

1. **Adopt the standard through the MWAA Design Manual and Tenant Design Criteria before Concourse C/D 30% design lock** (inside the $20–22.5B Dulles program's early-2027 design window). Board resolution alone is insufficient; the standard must appear in enforceable procurement and tenant instruments.
2. **Frame the DCA half around safety-communication preservation**, explicitly citing the post-January-2025 emergency-communication baseline. This defuses the Congressional and NTSB political overhang. Any DCA proposal that reads as "fewer announcements" without a paired safety-redundancy argument will not survive the first delegation phone call.
3. **Bank a Section 106 precedent on a non-historic space first** — Concourse E post-opening tuning is the ideal proving ground because it is greenfield and outside historic envelope — before proposing any work inside the Saarinen Main Terminal or DCA T1.

Everything else — PFC uplifts, federal rulemaking, statutory change — is background. The politics are not what will decide this. The Design Manual and the 2025 U&L will.

